                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is a Turnover Certificate for Current Account?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>A formal document issued by a practicing Chartered Accountant certifying the total annual turnover of your business, submitted to a bank as proof of financial activity when opening or upgrading a current account. It must carry a UDIN and is based on your GST returns or income tax returns.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Is GST registration mandatory to open a current account?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>No. Businesses below the GST threshold (Rs 40 lakh for goods, Rs 20 lakh for services) can submit a CA-issued Turnover Certificate instead. Banks accept this as valid proof of business activity under their board-approved KYC policies aligned with RBI guidelines.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Who can issue a Turnover Certificate for Current Account?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Only a practicing Chartered Accountant (CA) holding a full-time Certificate of Practice from ICAI. The certificate must include the CA firm name, Firm Registration Number (FRN), membership number, and a valid UDIN. Certificates without UDIN are invalid and will be rejected.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is UDIN and why is it mandatory?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>UDIN (Unique Document Identification Number) is an 18-digit code generated by the CA on the ICAI portal (udin.icai.org) for every certificate. Mandatory since 1 February 2019, it allows banks and regulators to instantly verify that the certificate is genuine and was issued by a qualified, practicing CA.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
